int rainbow copies of the complete d-ary tree\nwith 𝓁 levels, where d≥ 3. Let Ti be rooted at xi, Li =L(Ti) for i = 1, 2, and\nm(T1,T 2) =|{(v,w )∈L1×L2 :P (v,T 1)∪P (w,T 2) is rainbow}|.\nLet\nκ𝓁,d = min\nT1,T2\n{m(T1,T 2)}.\nThen,\nκ𝓁,d≥d2𝓁/4. (3)\nProof. We prove that\nκ𝓁,d≥\n(\n1−\n𝓁∑\ni=1\ni\ndi\n)\nd2𝓁≥d2𝓁/4.\nWe prove this by induction on 𝓁. If 𝓁 = 1, then clearly\nκ1,d =d(d− 1).\n4\n\n--- PAGE 5 ---\nSuppose that (3) holds for an 𝓁≥ 2.\nLet T1,T 2 be rainbow trees of height 𝓁 + 1. Moreover, let T′\n1 = T1\\L(T1) and\nT′\n2 =T2\\L(T2). We show that\nm(T1,T 2)≥d2·m(T′\n1,T′\n2)− (𝓁 + 1)d𝓁+1. (4)\nEach (v′,w′)∈ L′\n1×L′\n2 gives rise to d2 pairs of leaves (v,w )∈ L1×L2, where v′ is\nthe parent of v andw′ is the parent of w. Hence, the term d2·m(T′\n1,T′\n2) accounts for\nthe pairs (v,w ), where Pv′,T′\n1∪Pw′,T′\n2 is rainbow. We need to subtract oﬀ those pairs\nfor which Pv,T1∪Pw,T2 is not rainbow. Suppose that this number is ν. Let v∈L(T1)\nand letv′ be its parent, and let c be the color of the edge (v,v′). Then Pv,T1∪Pw,T2 is\nrainbow unless c is the color of some edge of Pw,T2. Now let ν(c) denote the number\nof root to leaf paths in T2 that contain an edge color c. Thus,\nν≤\n∑\nc\nν(c),\nwhere the summation is taken over all colors c that appear in edges of T1 adjacent to\nleaves. We bound this sum trivially, by summing over all colors in T2 (i.e., over all\nedges in T2, since T2 is rainbow). Note that if the depth of the edge colored c in T2\nis i, then ν(c)≤d𝓁+1−i. Thus, summing over edges of T2 gives us\n∑\nc\nν(c)≤\n𝓁+1∑\ni=1\nd𝓁+1−i·di = (𝓁 + 1)d𝓁+1,\nand consequently (4) holds. Thus, by induction (applied to T′\n1 and T′\n2)\nm(T1,T 2)≥d2·m(T′\n1,T′\n2)− (𝓁 + 1)d𝓁+1\n≥d2\n(\n1−\n𝓁∑\ni=1\ni\ndi\n)\nd2𝓁− (𝓁 + 1)d𝓁+1\n≥\n(\n1−\n𝓁+1∑\ni=1\ni\ndi\n)\nd2(𝓁+1),\nas required.\nIn the proof of Theorem 1 we will need a stronger version of the above lemma.\nLemma 3 LetT1,T 2 be two vertex disjoint edge colored copies of the complete d-ary\ntree with L levels, where d≥ 3. For i = 1, 2, let Ti be rooted at xi and suppose that\n5\n\n--- PAGE 6 ---\nedgese,f of Ti have a diﬀerent color whenever the distance between e and f in Ti is\nat most L. Let κ𝓁,d be as deﬁned in Lemma 2. Then\nκL,d≥\n\n1− L2\nd⌊L/2⌋−\n⌊L/2⌋∑\ni=1\ni\ndi\n\nd2L.\nProof. LetT𝓁\ni be the subtree of Ti spanned by the ﬁrst 𝓁 levels, where 1≤𝓁≤L and\ni = 1, 2. We show by induction on 𝓁 that\nm(T𝓁\n1,T 𝓁\n2)≥\n\n1− 𝓁2\nd⌊L/2⌋−\n⌊L/2⌋∑\ni=1\ni\ndi\n\nd2𝓁. (5)\nObserve ﬁrst that Lemma 2 implies (5) for 1 ≤ 𝓁≤⌊ L/2⌋− 1, since in this case T𝓁\n1\nand T𝓁\n2 must be rainbow.\nSuppose that⌊L/2⌋≤ 𝓁<L and consider the case where T1,T 2 have height𝓁 + 1.\nFollowing the argument of Lemma 2 we observe that color c can be the color of at\nmost d𝓁+1−⌊L/2⌋ leaf edges of T1. This is because for two leaf edges to have the same\ncolor, their common ancestor must be at distance (from the root) at most 𝓁−⌊L/2⌋.\nTherefore,\nm(T𝓁+1\n1 ,T 𝓁+1\n2 )≥d2·m(T𝓁\n1,T 𝓁\n2)−d𝓁+1−⌊L/2⌋\n∑\nc\nν(c)\n≥d2·m(T𝓁\n1,T 𝓁\n2)−d𝓁+1−⌊L/2⌋(𝓁 + 1)d𝓁+1\n=d2·m(T𝓁\n1,T 𝓁\n2)− (𝓁 + 1)d2(𝓁+1)−⌊L/2⌋.\nThus, by induction\nm(T𝓁+1\n1 ,T 𝓁+1\n2 )≥d2\n\n1− 𝓁2\nd⌊L/2⌋−\n⌊L/2⌋∑\ni=1\ni\ndi\n\nd2𝓁− (𝓁 + 1)d2(𝓁+1)−⌊L/2⌋\n=\n\n1− 𝓁2 +𝓁 + 1\nd⌊L/2⌋ −\n⌊L/2⌋∑\ni=1\ni\ndi\n\nd2(𝓁+1)\n≥\n\n1− (𝓁 + 1)2\nd⌊L/2⌋ −\n⌊L/2⌋∑\ni=1\ni\ndi\n\nd2(𝓁+1)\nyielding (5) and consequently the statement of the lemma.\n6\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
