{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_58","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"source .venv/bin/activate && python - <<'PY'\nfrom pypdf import PdfReader\np='papers/pages/1311.2299_rainbow_connection_random_regular_chunk_006.pdf'\nfor i,x in enumerate(PdfReader(p).pages,16): print(f'\\\\n--- PAGE {i} ---\\\\n{x.extract_text()}')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\n--- PAGE 16 ---\nPart of this can be handled by the sandwiching results of Kim and Vu [14] (see\nalso [9]).\nAcknowledgement We are grateful to Noga Alon for help on the case d = 3.\nReferences\n[1] N. Alon, private communication.\n[2] M. Basavaraju, L. S. Chandran, D. Rajendraprasad, and A. Ramaswamy, Rain-\nbow connection number and radius , Graphs Combin. 30, no. 2, 275–285.\n[3] B. Bollob´ as,A probabilistic proof of an asymptotic formula for the number of\nlabelled regular graphs, European J. Combin. 1 (1980), no. 4, 311–316.\n[4] , Random graphs, second ed., Cambridge Studies in Advanced Mathemat-\nics, vol. 73, Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 2001.\n[5] Y. Caro, A. Lev, Y. Roditty, Z. Tuza, and R. Yuster, On rainbow connection,\nElectron. J. Combin. 15 (2008), no. 1, Research paper 57, 13.\n[6] S. Chakraborty, E. Fischer, A. Matsliah, and R. Yuster, Hardness and algorithms\nfor rainbow connection, J. Comb. Optim. 21 (2011), no. 3, 330–347.\n[7] L. S. Chandran, A. Das, D. Rajendraprasad, and N.M. Varma, Rainbow connec-\ntion number and connected dominating sets , J. Graph Theory 71 (2012), no. 2,\n206–218.\n[8] G. Chartrand, G. L. Johns, K. A. McKeon, and P. Zhang, Rainbow connection\nin graphs, Math. Bohem. 133 (2008), no. 1, 85–98.\n[9] A. Dudek, A. Frieze, A. Ruci´ nski, and M.ˇSileikis, EmbeddingG(n,m ) inG(n,d )\nwith some edges forbidden , to appear.\n[10] A. Frieze and C. E. Tsourakakis, Rainbow connectivity of sparse random graphs,\nApproximation, randomization, and combinatorial optimization, Lecture Notes\nin Comput. Sci., vol. 7408, Springer, Heidelberg, 2012, pp. 54